0,0],\n [0,7,0],\n [0,0,7],\n [7,0,7]]\n\nExample 5: 3x6 color 8.\nInput:\n[[8,8,8,5,5,8],\n [5,5,8,8,5,8],\n [8,5,8,5,8,5]]\nOutput: 8->0,5->8:\n[[0,0,0,8,8,0],\n [8,8,0,0,8,0],\n [0,8,0,8,0,8]]\n\nExample 6: 1x? need rectangular? 1 row fine. Color 6.\nInput: [[6,5,6,5]]\nOutput: [[0,6,0,6]]\n\nExample 7: maybe fully one color? No grey boxes? verifier works if non-five color exists and no 5? x5 empty, x6 fill x4 with x2 (no cells) returns I with color->0. But rule \"replace grey boxes\" maybe no. Could be confusing. Avoid.\n\nAlso include color 9 perhaps.\n\nWe can call show_transformed_input multiple in parallel. Then finish_teaching. The environment will compute outputs.\n\nLet's pick 6 diverse examples. Should we include the teacher example? It was provided, we can also show it maybe. The Student hasn't seen it. We can include or not. Including it is fine; it is a valid pair. But perhaps better to use it as one of demos. I'll include it too, plus others. Since more examples help. Let's do 6-7.\n\nWe need ensure all inputs rectangular, ints 0-9. Good.\n\nLet's submit in one block: multiple calls. Then finish.\n\nActually \"You may submit a different one\" if refused. We'll see outputs. Then call finish_teaching.\n\nLet's do 8 examples to cover colors 1-9 except 5 and various shapes. Not too many.\n\nPlan inputs:\n1) teacher example 3x3 color 1.\n2) 3x3 color 2 as above.\n3) 4x4 color 3.\n4) 2x5 color 4.\n5) 5x3 color 7.\n6) 3x6 color 8.\n7) 1x6 color 6.\n8) 3x3 color 9.\n\nCheck each is evaluable: palette includes 5 and one other. first(remove(5, palette)) yields that color.\n\nLet's call all.",
